<section class="cxx entity object">
<h2>ByteArrayFactory and storage</h2>
<p><code>Arcadia_ByteArrayFactory_createImmutable</code> wraps an immutable
<code>Arcadia_RuntimeByteArray</code> without copying and keeps the runtime value
reachable through GC traversal. The immutable runtime value remains distinct
from the object-level <code>Arcadia_ByteArray</code>.</p>
<p><code>Arcadia_ByteArrayFactory_createMutable</code> creates mutable,
fixed-size contiguous native storage; <code>createResizable</code> creates mutable,
resizable contiguous native storage. Both zero-initialize their requested size.
<code>copyMutable</code> and <code>copyResizable</code> make independent copies of
contiguous ByteArray sources. <code>copyImmutable</code> creates a separate
immutable runtime-value-backed snapshot; it never aliases the source, even if
its capability queries report immutable. Non-contiguous sources are rejected
by these initial copy APIs.</p>
<p>The factory-owned writable storage is deallocated with its object. A borrow
from <code>Arcadia_ByteArray_getBytes</code> is native C-only and valid only while
the underlying object and its storage are alive; resizing can relocate or
invalidate a borrowed pointer. The returned pointer must not be stored in
PDL values. Clients must root managed objects while borrowing their storage.</p>
<p>Fixed-size mutable storage supports <code>setAt</code> but rejects
<code>resize</code> with <code>Arcadia_Status_OperationInvalid</code> even when
the requested size equals the current size. Mutable writes outside the current
bounds raise <code>Arcadia_Status_ArgumentValueInvalid</code>. Growable storage
zeroes newly exposed elements, including after shrinking and regrowing.</p>
<p>Legacy slices continue as fixed-size read-only views; this phase does not
introduce writable slice forwarding or altered reader cursor behavior.
<code>ByteArrayBuilder</code> and all three byte-reader implementations remain
available for compatibility during the staged consolidation.</p>
</section>
